  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 122 AM CDT Sat Aug 29 2026\n\nAn upper level ridge over the southern Rockies will migrate\neastward across the southern plains and Missouri/Arkansas \nregion over the next week. Thus, the northwesterly mid level \nflow of late that has helped promote t-storm activity will end. \nMid level disturbances will move over the ridge across the \nnorthern plains. These disturbances will generally help \nestablish a frontal zone across northern Kansas and southern \nNebraska by early next week. For the next two afternoons, mid \nlevel zonal flow across the Rockies will result in a surface \ntrough in the lee of the Rockies. Isolated high-based t-storms \nwill develop along the trough and may impact extreme western \nKansas. Given the expected marginal instability, these storms \nshould be sub- severe, with 40-55 mph wind gusts possible from \nJohnson to Syracuse Saturday and Sunday evenings. Very hot \nweather can be expected, with highs from 100-105. Given the dry \nair in place, heat indices will be at or below the actual \ntemperatures. \n\nHot weather is expected to persist through next week as the \naforementioned upper level ridge persists across the southern \nUnited States. Any chance for t-storms will be across far \nwestern Kansas and along I-70, with rain chances near 0% over \nsouth central Kansas. Temperatures are forecast to meet or \nexceed 100 degrees each day, except perhaps some upper 90s over\nwest central Kansas. The various ensemble means indicate the \nupper level ridge only gradually passing eastward and flattening\nout. This pattern would keep intense heat in place through next\nweekend, with little in the way of rainfall. The grand ensemble\nshows 0-5% chances of daily 24 hour rainfalls exceeding a \nquarter of an inch through 5 September, with the 5% closer to \nthe Colorado state line and along I-70.",
